WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:02.640
Have you ever wondered

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:06.560
if there are more natural ways
to lower your high blood pressure,

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.520
guard against Alzheimer's,
lose weight, and feel better?

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.720
Well, it turns out there is!

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:15.200
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.160
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.600
Today, we learn a little bit more
about how to keep our heart healthy.

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:25.680
And we start with ways to
improve our heart rate variability.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.400
Everyone feel for your pulse.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:30.240
Hold out your hand, thumb up,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.680
and feel for the knobby bone
at the top of your wrist.

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:35.760
Okay, now,
slide your hand down

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.920
and across the front
of your wrist.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:40.720
Feel those, like,
strands of spaghetti?

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.120
Those are the tendons
in your wrist.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.760
If you press lightly
between the knobby bone

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.280
and that first tendon,
you should feel your pulse.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:50.640
Got it?

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.000
Okay,
now what we're going to do

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.320
is feel what happens
to our pulse

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.240
when we take a deep breath.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.600
We're going to try it twice.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.480
Try to feel what happens
to your pulse when you inhale

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.720
and when you exhale.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.680
All right, here we go,
slow inhale.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.280
(inhales)

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.440
Slow exhale.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:11.200
(exhales)

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:12.960
One more time.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.280
In.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.560
Out.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:23.440
What happened when you
breathed in and breathed out?

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:26.960
You should have felt your pulse
speed up when you inhaled

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.680
and slow down
when you exhaled.

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:32.640
That's called
heart rate variability,

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:34.240
a measure of the control

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.200
our vagus nerve has
over our heart.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.560
And it's a good thing.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.080
A healthy heart
is not a metronome.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:44.800
Low heart rate variability
predicts greater risk

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:47.760
of heart disease and death
from several causes.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.880
High-risk patients with
low variability in heart rate

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:54.000
have twice the risk
of dying prematurely.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:55.200
Is there anything we can do

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.800
to positively impact
heart rate variability?

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:03.600
Voluntary slow breathing,
at about six breaths per minute,

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.960
is a technique that's been used
for thousands of years

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.000
as an essential part

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.360
of many meditative
and relaxation practices.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.800
It can benefit several heart
rate variability parameters,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:19.120
offering a low-tech,
low-cost technique.

00:02:19.120 --> 00:02:22.800
Speaking of low-tech, low-cost:
What about exercise?

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.800
Aerobic training
at least twice a week

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.240
positively influences
heart nerve control.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:30.640
What about what we eat?

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:32.560
Those eating plant-based diets

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:35.120
have better
overall heart rate variability.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:37.600
Vegetarians, for example,

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.200
don't tend to have only
better blood pressure,

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.040
cholesterol, triglycerides,
and blood sugars,

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:46.320
they also have
better heart rate variability.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.400
The vagus nerve toning effects
of plant-based diets

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.160
may help explain part of
their cardioprotective effect.

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:56.080
Any plants in particular?

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.320
While no significant association
was seen

00:02:58.320 --> 00:02:59.920
between heart rate
variability measures

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.960
and the intake of fruits
and vegetables in general,

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.000
the consumption
of green leafy vegetables

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:07.120
seems to stand out,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.240
perhaps helping to explain

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.680
why eating just a half serving
of greens a day

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:15.680
may cut our risk of having a
heart attack by up to 67%!

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:26.800
In our next story,
How Not to Die from heart disease.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.160
The most likely reason most of our
loved ones will die is heart disease.

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:35.120
It's still up to each of us to make
our own decisions as to what to eat

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:37.840
and how to live — but we should
make these choices consciously,

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.480
educating ourselves about the predictable
consequences of our actions.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.840
Atherosclerosis, hardening of
the arteries, begins in childhood.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.840
By age 10, the arteries of nearly all kids
raised on the standard American diet

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.440
already have fatty streaks —
the first stage of the disease.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:59.200
Then the plaques start
forming in our 20s,

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:03.760
get worse in our 30s and
then can start killing us off.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.787
In our heart it's called a heart attack;
in our brain it can manifest as a stroke.

00:04:07.787 --> 00:04:07.840
So if there is anyone watching
this older than age 10,

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:11.200
then the choice isn't whether or not
to eat healthy to prevent heart disease,

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.880
it's whether or not you want to reverse
the heart disease you likely already have.

00:04:17.520 --> 00:04:19.440
Is that even possible?

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.480
When researchers took people with
heart disease and put them on

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:24.560
the kind of plant-based diet
followed by populations

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:26.880
that did not get epidemic
heart disease,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:33.520
their hope was that it might slow the disease
process down, maybe even stop it.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.360
But instead something
miraculous happened.

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.560
The disease started to reverse,
to get better.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.200
As soon as patients stopped
eating artery-clogging diets,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.920
their bodies were able to start
dissolving some of the plaque away,

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:48.800
opening up arteries without
drugs, without surgery,

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:51.840
suggesting their bodies
wanted to heal all along,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:55.488
but were just never
given the chance.

00:04:55.488 --> 00:04:55.558
That improvement in blood flow to
the heart muscle itself (on the left)

00:04:55.558 --> 00:04:55.600
was after just three weeks eating healthy.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.480
Let me share with you what's been
called the best kept secret in medicine.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.640
The best kept secret in medicine is that
sometimes, given the right conditions,

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:08.160
the body can heal itself.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.680
You know if you whack your shin
really hard on a coffee table,

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:15.040
it can get all red, hot, painful,
swollen, inflamed,

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.080
but will heal naturally if
you just stand back

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:20.800
and let your body work its magic.

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:25.680
But what if you kept whacking your
shin in the same place day after day,

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:29.280
in fact three times a day
(breakfast, lunch, and dinner).

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.240
It'd never heal!

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.880
You'd go to your doctor and
be like, "Oh, my shin hurts,"

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:35.600
and the doctor would be like,
"No problem," whip out their pad,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.480
write you a prescription
for painkillers.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.480
You're still whacking your
shin three times a day.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:41.920
Oh, it still really hurts like heck,

00:05:41.920 --> 00:05:45.520
but oh, feels so much better
with those pain pills on board.

00:05:45.520 --> 00:05:49.760
Thank heavens for
modern medicine.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.960
It's like when people take nitroglycerine
for crushing chest pain,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.440
tremendous relief, but you're not doing
anything to treat the underlying cause.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.040
Our body wants to come
back to health if we let it.

00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:07.680
But if we keep re-damaging ourselves
three times a day, we may never heal.

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.400
One of the most amazing things
I learned in all my medical training

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.800
was that within about 15 years
of stopping smoking,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:17.520
your lung cancer risk approaches
that of a lifelong nonsmoker.

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:18.880
Isn't that amazing?

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.760
Your lungs can clear
out all that tar,

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:25.040
and eventually, it's almost, as if
you never started smoking at all.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.320
And every morning of our smoking
life that healing process started until —

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:33.920
Wham! Our first cigarette of the day,
reinjuring our lungs with every puff,

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:37.440
just like we can reinjure our
arteries with every bite,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.880
when all we had to do all
along—the miracle cure—

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:45.840
is just stand back, get out of the way,
stop re-damaging ourselves,

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:51.680
and let our bodies natural healing
processes bring us back towards health.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:56.880
The human body is a
self-healing machine.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:02.480
Sure, you could choose moderation—
hit yourself with a smaller hammer—

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:06.720
but why beat yourself up at all?

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:09.600
I don't tell my smoking patients
to cut down to half-a-pack a day,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.040
I tell them to quit.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.680
Sure, a half pack is better
than two packs a day,

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:18.240
but we should try to only put
healthy things in our mouths.

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.360
We've known about this for decades:
American Heart Journal, 1977.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.240
Cases like Mr. F.W. here —
heart disease so bad

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:31.280
he couldn't even make it to the mailbox,
but started eating healthier,

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:34.897
and a few months later he was
climbing mountains, no pain.

00:07:34.897 --> 00:07:34.965
There are these fancy new anti-angina,
anti-chest pain drugs out now.

00:07:34.965 --> 00:07:35.003
They cost thousands of dollars a year,

00:07:35.003 --> 00:07:35.073
but at the highest dose, they may be
able to prolong exercise duration,

00:07:35.073 --> 00:07:35.106
as long as 33 and a half seconds.

00:07:35.106 --> 00:07:35.156
It does not look like those
choosing the drug route

00:07:35.156 --> 00:07:35.195
will be climbing mountains
anytime soon.

00:07:35.195 --> 00:07:37.120
See, plant-based diets aren't
just safer and cheaper.

00:07:37.120 --> 00:07:43.600
They can work better because you're
treating the actual cause of the disease.

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:45.200
Finally today,

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:49.680
when doctors withhold dietary treatment
options from cardiac patients,

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:55.040
they are violating the cornerstone of
medical ethics, informed consent.

00:07:55.040 --> 00:07:57.440
When he was a surgeon at
the Cleveland Clinic,

00:07:57.440 --> 00:08:02.640
Dr. Caldwell Esselstyn published a controversial
paper in the American Journal of Cardiology.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.840
Heart bypass operations
carry significant risks,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.400
including the potential to cause further
heart damage, stroke, brain dysfunction.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:14.560
Angioplasty isn’t much better, also carrying
significant mortality and morbidity,

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.600
and often doesn’t work, in
terms of decreasing risk

00:08:17.600 --> 00:08:20.000
of subsequent heart
attack or death.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.560
So, it seems we have
an enormous paradox.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.040
The disease that is the leading
killer of men and women

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.280
in Western civilization
is largely untreated.

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.040
The benefits of the invasive procedures
are at best temporary,

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:37.280
with most patients eventually
succumbing to their disease.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:40.800
In cancer we call
that palliative care,

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.080
where we just kind of
throw up our hands,

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:45.920
throw in the towel and give up actually
trying to treat the disease.

00:08:45.920 --> 00:08:49.760
So why does this juggernaut of
invasive procedures persist?

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:52.320
Well one reason is that performing
surgical interventions

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:56.640
has the potential for
enormous financial reward.

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.480
That’s considered one of the barriers to the&nbsp;

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:02.080
practice of preventive cardiology -
adequate return.

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:06.240
Diet and lifestyle interventions
loses money for the physician.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.680
Although the practice of preventive
cardiology is not as lucrative,

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:12.480
this article was hoping to nudge
cardiologists in that direction

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.520
by appealing to less
tangible benefits.

00:09:15.520 --> 00:09:19.600
Another barrier is doctors don’t
think patients want it.

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.720
Physician surveys show that doctors often
don’t even bring up diet and lifestyle options,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.640
assuming that patients
would prefer for example

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.320
to be on cholesterol-lowering drugs
every day for the rest of their lives.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.280
That may be true for some, but
it’s up to the patient to decide.

00:09:35.280 --> 00:09:38.320
According to the official
AMA Code of Medical Ethics,

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:43.440
physicians are supposed to disclose all
relevant medical information to patients.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:47.840
The patient’s right of self-decision
can be effectively exercised

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.320
only if the patient possesses enough
information to enable an informed choice.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:57.680
The physician's obligation is to present the
medical facts accurately to the patient.

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:02.080
For example, before starting someone at moderate
risk on a cholesterol-lowering statin drug,

00:10:02.080 --> 00:10:05.760
a physician might ideally
say something like

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.920
“You should know that for
folks in your situation,

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.640
the number of individuals who
must be treated with a statin

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:14.560
to prevent one death from a cardiovascular
event such as a heart attack or stroke—

00:10:14.560 --> 00:10:17.040
is generally between
60 and 100,

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:20.480
which means that if I treated
60 people in your position,

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:23.040
1 might benefit
and 59 would not.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.760
As these numbers show, it is
important for you to know

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:30.000
that most of the people who take a
statin will not benefit from doing so

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.160
and, moreover, that statins
can have side effects,

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:34.880
such as muscle pain, liver
damage, upset stomach,

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.480
even in people who do not
benefit from the medication.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.080
I am giving you this information so that
you can weigh the risks and benefits

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:46.640
of drugs versus diet and then
make an informed decision.”

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.280
Yet how many physicians
have these kinds

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.120
of frank and open discussions
with their patients?

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:55.920
Non-disclosure of medical
information by doctors,

00:10:55.920 --> 00:10:58.720
that kind of paternalism is supposed
to be a thing of the past,

00:10:58.720 --> 00:11:03.600
but now physicians are supposed
to honor informed consent,

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.040
unless the patient's in a coma
or something or it's an emergency.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.760
But too many physicians continue
to treat their patients

00:11:09.760 --> 00:11:13.520
as if…they were unconscious.

00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:18.400
At the end of this long roundtable
discussion on angioplasty and stents,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.960
the editor-in-chief of the
American Journal of Cardiology

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:26.240
reminded the participants of an
important fact to place it all in context.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:28.640
Atherosclerosis is
due to high cholesterol,

00:11:28.640 --> 00:11:30.320
which is due to poor
dietary choices,

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.600
and so if we all existed
on a plant-based diet,

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:39.200
we would not have even
needed this discussion.

